positive_text:
Let $T=\{q_{1} q_{2} \cdots q_{k} \mid q_{1}, q_{2}, \ldots, q_{k}$ are distinct primes with $q_{i} < 30$ for all $i$\}$. Assume $1$ is in $T$. For an initial value $b_{1} \in T$, define the sequence $b_{n}$ by the rule:
$$
\begin{aligned}
b_{n+1} = \begin{cases} 
\dfrac{b_{n}}{n+1}, & \text{if } b_{n} \text{ is divisible by } n+1 \\
(n+2) b_{n}, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
$$
How many values of $b_{1}$ in $T$ satisfy $b_{j} = b_{1}$ for infinitely many positive integers $j$?

negatives.text:
Let $S = \{\prod_{i=1}^{n} p_i \mid p_i$ are distinct primes with $p_i < 30$\}$, including $1$. Define a sequence $a_n$ such that:
$$
a_{n+1} = 
\begin{cases} 
\dfrac{a_n}{n+1}, & (n+1) \mid a_n \\
(n+2)^2 a_n, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
$$
How many values of $a_1$ in $S$ yield a sequence $a_n$ where $a_j = a_1$ for infinitely many $j$?

Let $S = \{\prod_{i=1}^{n} p_i \mid p_i$ are distinct primes with $p_i < 30$\}$, including $1$. Define a sequence $a_n$ such that:
$$
a_{n+1} = 
\begin{cases} 
\dfrac{a_n}{n+1}, & (n+1) \mid a_n \\
(n+2)a_n, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
$$
How many values of $a_1$ in $S$ yield a sequence $a_n$ where $a_j = a_1$ for some $j > 1$?

